There's no search input in this directory, so here it is in plain JavaScript; swap in your own selector and API call.

```js
const input = document.querySelector('#search');
let timer;
let controller;

input.addEventListener('input', () => {
  clearTimeout(timer);
  timer = setTimeout(async () => {
    controller?.abort(); // cancel the in-flight request so stale results can't overwrite newer ones
    controller = new AbortController();
    try {
      const res = await fetch(`/api/search?q=${encodeURIComponent(input.value)}`, {
        signal: controller.signal,
      });
      render(await res.json());
    } catch (err) {
      if (err.name !== 'AbortError') throw err;
    }
  }, 300);
});
```

It waits 300 ms after the last keystroke before calling the API, and `render` stands in for whatever you do with the results today. If this is React, the same timer goes in a `useEffect` keyed on the query with `clearTimeout` in the cleanup; tell me the framework and I'll show that version.
